.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt BOCHS 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bochs ,
.Nm bochs-drm
.Nd DRM driver of QEMU's standard VGA
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/drm_mode.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver binds the display adapter of the Bochs DISPI interface, QEMU's
.Ql -vga std ,
.Ql -device VGA
and
.Ql -device bochs-display ,
whose PCI id is 1234:1111, and the same adapter under 4321:1111, as
Linux 6.12's bochs-drm does.
It needs at least 4 MiB of video memory in BAR 0, and reads the DISPI
registers through BAR 2 when the device has it and through the ports
0x1ce and 0x1cf otherwise.
A device whose DISPI id is not 0xb0c0 to 0xb0cf is not bound.
.Cm nomodeset
or
.Cm bochs.modeset=0
on the kernel command line keeps the driver off, and the screen goes to
.Xr fbdrm 4 .
.Pp
Before it touches the device the driver removes the firmware's framebuffer
from every memory BAR the device decodes, so that
.Xr fbdrm 4
leaves the screen to it and the machine has one card.
A bound device is a DRM device, as
.Xr drm 4
describes, registered as
.Ql bochs-drm
with Linux's identity: description
.Ql bochs dispi vga interface (qemu stdvga) ,
version 1.0.0 and date 20130925, on the PCI bus.
It has a card node alone, and the features
.Dv DRIVER_GEM ,
.Dv DRIVER_MODESET
and
.Dv DRIVER_ATOMIC .
The kernel logs
.Dq bochs: registered
with the DISPI id, the video memory, the registers' place, whether QEMU
gave an EDID, and the card.
.Ss Display
The device has one CRTC, one primary plane of formats XRGB8888 and
BGRX8888, one encoder of type
.Dv DRM_MODE_ENCODER_NONE
and one connector of type
.Dv DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_VIRTUAL ,
.Ql Virtual-1 ,
always connected.
.Pp
The connector's modes come from the EDID QEMU puts at the start of BAR 2,
which it gives unless the device has
.Ql edid=off ;
the connector then has the
.Ql EDID
property, and QEMU's
.Ql xres
and
.Ql yres
name the preferred mode, 1280x800 by default.
Without an EDID the modes are the DMT modes up to 8192x8192, with
1024x768 preferred.
Either way a mode is listed and accepted only when two frames of it at 32
bits fit in video memory, so that one can be shown while the other is
drawn: with QEMU's default 16 MiB, 1920x1080 fits and 1920x1200 does
not, and
.Ql vgamem_mb
raises the limit.
.Pp
A mode set writes the DISPI registers as Linux's driver does: 32 bits a
pixel, the mode's size, and a virtual height of the whole of video memory.
The plane's buffer is shown through the virtual width and the X and Y
offsets, which a page flip alone writes, so a flip moves no pixels.
XRGB8888 sets the little-endian byte order in QEMU's extended registers,
and BGRX8888 the big-endian one, on a device that has them.
Turning the CRTC off blanks the screen through the VGA attribute
controller.
.Ss Buffers
Dumb buffers have rows of the width's bytes, unpadded, and a whole number
of pages.
A buffer is made in video memory when there is room, from the top down,
and in system memory otherwise.
Each buffer a commit shows is pinned in video memory: moved there first,
with the buffers no commit shows moved out to system memory to make room,
the least recently shown first.
A mapping follows its buffer: it is write-combining, and after a move the
next access reaches the buffer's new place.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/dri/cardN
.It Pa /dev/dri/cardN
The card node of a bound device.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
Besides those of
.Xr drm 4 :
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EINVAL
A mode whose two frames do not fit in video memory; an import of another
device's buffer through
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE .
.It Er ENOMEM
A commit whose buffer finds no room in video memory beside those already
shown.
The display is left as it was.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr fbdrm 4 ,
.Xr drm-kms 7
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux 6.12's bochs-drm and its VRAM helper, whose
register sequences, mode limits and buffer placement it takes; it places
buffers from the top of video memory where TTM places them from the
bottom.
